# Blog river posts

The homepage is a chronological stream. Write the opening so the feed is useful without opening the permalink.

## Choose a type

- `entry` (default): an essay. The river shows the first markdown block (or everything before `<!--more-->`) plus `[... N words]`.
- `link`: an annotated URL. Set `linkUrl`. The river shows the **full** body; the title points at the source.

```yaml
type: link
title: "Concrete title of the thing you're linking"
linkUrl: https://example.com/post
viaUrl: https://optional-source.example   # optional
viaTitle: Optional label
project: tinysh                          # optional, must match projects.config.ts
tags: [shells, c]                        # optional, freeform
```

## Entries

- First paragraph is the briefing: what this is, why it matters, with real links.
- Do not open with a teaser that only makes sense after a click.
- Use `<!--more-->` in `.md` (or `{/* more */}` in `.mdx`) when the first paragraph is a series note and you want a richer slice in the river.
- `description` is SEO/RSS, not the river excerpt.

## Links

- Publish whenever something is worth pointing at. A few sentences of commentary is enough.
- Keep nested links, code, and lists in the body — they stay live in the feed.
- Still give the post a local slug; the date in the river is the permalink.

## Tags vs project

- `project`: at most one, a slug from `src/projects.config.ts`. Powers `/blog/project/<slug>` and homepage chips.
- `tags`: extra facets (`/blog/tag/<tag>`). Use lowercase, hyphenated, sparingly (topic, not every keyword).
- Full playbook: `BLOGGING.md`.
